.TH SHIMBACK 1 "2026-09-12" "shimback 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
shimback \- run a primary command, transparently fall back to another
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shimback add
.I name
[\fB\-s\fR \fIsource\fR] \fB\-f\fR \fIfallback\fR
[\fB\-\-policy\fR \fIexit\-code\fR|\fIheuristic\fR|\fIexit\-code\-match\fR|\fIroute\-args\fR]
[\fB\-\-error\-pattern\fR \fIp\fR]\|.\|.\|. [\fB\-\-exit\-code\fR \fIcode\fR]\|.\|.\|.
[\fB\-\-route\-arg\fR \fIarg\fR]\|.\|.\|. [\fB\-\-strip\-matched\-args\fR] [\fB\-\-diagnostic\fR]
.br
.B shimback remove
.I name
.br
.B shimback init
.br
.B shimback list
(alias:
.BR ls )
.br
.B shimback doctor
.br
.B shimback install
[\fB\-\-prefix\fR \fIdir\fR]
.br
.B shimback uninstall
[\fB\-\-prefix\fR \fIdir\fR] [\fB\-\-full\fR]
.br
.B shimback \-\-help
|
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shimback
is a small, dependency\-free command\-line shim: it wraps a command name
(e.g.
.IR sed )
with a
.B source
binary to run and a
.B fallback
binary to transparently retry with if the source doesn't work out.
.PP
.B add
creates a symlink named
.I name
pointing at the
.B shimback
binary itself, inside a dedicated shim directory that's prepended to
.BR PATH .
When
.I name
is run, the OS finds that symlink first;
.B shimback
inspects
.IR argv[0] ,
sees it was invoked as
.I name
rather than
.BR shimback ,
looks up
.IR name 's
configuration, and runs the source command with the given arguments. If the
source command fails (the exact condition depends on the shim's policy, see
.B POLICIES
below),
.B shimback
transparently re\-runs the same arguments against the fallback command
instead. If the source succeeds, its output is passed through as if
.B shimback
weren't there at all.
.PP
A failed trial run of the source command is designed to be invisible: its
stdout and stderr are captured, not streamed live, and are discarded
entirely if a fallback is triggered \(em nothing about the failed attempt
reaches the terminal unless the shim explicitly opts into a one\-line
diagnostic (see
.B \-\-diagnostic
below).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "add " name " [\-s " source "] \-f " fallback " [options...]"
Create or update a shim named
.IR name ,
wrapping
.I source
(or, if
.B \-s
is omitted, whatever
.I name
resolves to fresh on
.B PATH
at dispatch time, skipping shimback's own shim directory) with
.I fallback
under the given policy. Refuses to create a shim where source and fallback
resolve to the same binary. Also ensures the shim directory is on
.B PATH
for the current shell, via an idempotent marker\-block injection into its
startup file.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback add sed \-f /usr/bin/sed
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.BI "remove " name
Remove
.IR name 's
symlink and config entry. Leaves the
.B PATH
injection in the shell startup file alone, since other shims (or a future
.BR add )
may still need it.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback remove sed
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B init
Detect every installed shell (zsh, bash, fish) and add the shim directory to
.B PATH
in each one's startup file(s), not just the current shell. fish is detected
but not automatically configured as of v0.1.0; a manual command is printed
instead.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback init
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B list
List every configured shim as a table: name, source (or
.IR auto ),
fallback, policy, and diagnostic flag. Also available as
.BR ls .
Colored when stdout is a terminal and
.B NO_COLOR
is unset.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback list
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B doctor
Check the health of the whole shimback setup: whether the shim directory
exists and is on
.BR PATH ,
whether the config file parses, and, for each shim, whether its symlink is
dead, whether its fallback/source still exist and are executable, and
whether its policy has everything it needs to ever trigger. Exits
.B 0
if everything checks out,
.B 1
otherwise.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback doctor
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.BI "install [\-\-prefix " dir "]"
Copy the running
.B shimback
binary to
.IR dir /bin/shimback
(default
.IR dir :
.IR \(ti/.local )
and ensure both
.IR dir /bin
and the shim directory (normally
.BR add / init 's
job) are on
.BR PATH .
Also installs this man page to
.IR dir /share/man/man1 ,
preferring a copy bundled next to the running binary and falling back to
downloading it from the shimback GitHub release matching the running
version if no local copy is found (requires
.BR curl ;
skipped with a warning, non\-fatally, if unavailable). Safe to re\-run after
building a newer version to refresh the installed copy.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback install \-\-prefix ~/.local
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.BI "uninstall [\-\-prefix " dir "] [\-\-full]"
Remove every symlink
.B shimback
created in the shim directory, the
.IR dir /bin/shimback
binary installed by
.BR install ,
and the man page installed alongside it. With
.BR \-\-full ,
also delete the config file and remove the
.B PATH
marker blocks from shell startup files; without it, the config and
.B PATH
blocks are left alone.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback uninstall \-\-full
.fi
.RE
.RE
.SH POLICIES
.TP
.B exit\-code
(the default) Fall back whenever the source command exits non\-zero.
.TP
.B heuristic
Only fall back when the source's stderr matches one of the shim's
configured
.B \-\-error\-pattern
values (case\-insensitive substring match). Requires at least one
.BR \-\-error\-pattern .
.TP
.B exit\-code\-match
Only fall back when the source exits with one of the shim's configured
.B \-\-exit\-code
values (repeatable, each 0\(en255). Any other non\-zero exit surfaces as\-is.
Requires at least one
.BR \-\-exit\-code .
.TP
.B route\-args
Not a fallback\-on\-failure policy: picks source or fallback
.I before
running either, based on whether any invocation argument exactly matches
one of the shim's configured
.B \-\-route\-arg
values (fallback if so, source if not), then runs only that one with live,
inherited stdio \(em no captured trial run, no retry on failure. Pass
.B \-\-strip\-matched\-args
to drop the matched argument(s) before forwarding the rest. Requires at
least one
.BR \-\-route\-arg .
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
By default, falling back leaves no trace. Pass
.B \-\-diagnostic
at
.B add
time (or set
.I diagnostic = true
in the config) to print a single line to stderr whenever that shim falls
back:
.RS
.nf
shimback: 'sed' failed; falling back to /usr/bin/sed
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/shimback/config.toml
Shim configuration (falls back to
.IR $HOME/.config/shimback/config.toml
if
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset). Plain, hand\-editable TOML, managed by
.B add
and
.BR remove .
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/shimback/bin
The shim directory: every shim's symlink lives here (falls back to
.IR $HOME/.local/share/shimback/bin ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 127
The shim, source, or fallback couldn't be found or isn't executable.
.TP
.B 1
A CLI or config validation error.
.TP
other
Passed straight through from whichever of source/fallback actually ran (or
128 + signal if it was killed by a signal).
.SH SEE ALSO
Full documentation and source:
.UR https://github.com/pnavais/shimback
.UE
.SH AUTHOR
Pablo Navais
